Cannot be established from this repository alone: depends on state only known at runtime (e.g. feature flags, request data).
The persistence mechanisms are explicit, but observable impact requires a settings save and depends on the runtime contents of the affected file—particularly whether it contains comments, environment-variable references, malformed JSON, arrays, or keys omitted from the update.

CLI settings persistence now updates existing comment-bearing JSON in place instead of replacing it with freshly serialized JSON. It tracks environment-variable references during load so later saves can write the references rather than resolved values, preserves existing keys and comments while applying updates, and leaves malformed existing files unchanged after logging an error.

Likely behavioral changes
  • Saving an existing settings file now overlays the current settings onto its parsed structure and serializes that structure with comment-aware tooling, so existing comments and fields outside the update are retained rather than the file being wholly regenerated.
  • For settings that were loaded from a standalone environment-variable reference, subsequent saves restore the original reference in the file instead of persisting the resolved runtime value.
  • If an existing settings file cannot be parsed, a save now reports the parse problem and returns without modifying that file.

